  "text": "Question seven. What is a medallion architecture: bronze, silver, gold? Three layers, in a lake or lakehouse. Bronze holds the raw source data, exactly as it arrived. Silver is cleaned, deduplicated and conformed, with joins applied. Gold holds business aggregates, shaped for a dashboard or a model. Each layer is rebuilt from the one below it: gold from silver, silver from bronze. So when a cleaning rule turns out wrong, fix it and rebuild: bronze still holds the original.\n\nQuestion eight. What is a Lambda versus Kappa architecture? Lambda runs two paths side by side: a batch layer, accurate over all of history, and a speed layer for fresh results. A serving layer merges them. The cost: every piece of logic is written twice, and kept in step. Kappa keeps one streaming path. To reprocess history, replay the event log, in Kafka say, through the same code. Kappa is simpler when the streaming engine can handle batch semantics, and the log reaches back far enough to replay. Lambda is the fallback when it can't.",
